<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 132 0 R
/E (In Belgium, people who apply for international protection at the border are systematically detained - and the border asylum procedure that follows moves fast, often too fast for people to access information, fully understand and exercise their rights, or receive quality legal assistance. The combination of detention conditions and accelerated procedures create significant barriers to effective access to international protection and can lead to breaches of fundamental rights. )
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
230 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 132 0 R
/E (For many years, NANSEN has monitored international protection procedures for persons held in administrative detention through regular visits to closed detention centres, individual casework, and rigorous legal analysis, in close cooperation with the MOVE Coalition and other partner organisations.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [2 442 0 R 4]
>>
endobj
231 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Span
/K [3]
/Pg 15 0 R
/P 442 0 R
>>
endobj
232 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 132 0 R
/E (What we found was troubling. NANSEN observed that information on the possibility of applying for international protection is not always provided at the border and that detention orders often rely on stereotypical justifications rather than individual assessments. NANSEN also identified concerns regarding the effective examination of non-refoulement. Legal safeguards intended to address these problems - appeals, judicial review - too often fail to provide a meaningful check.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
