/T (People who have experienced torture often face specific barriers when seeking international protection. The physical and psychological consequences of torture can shape how someone is able to recount and substantiate what happened to them. Medical evidence and a better understanding of the visible and less visible consequences of torture can be decisive in these cases, but only if it is properly gathered and given appropriate weight.)
/E (People who have experienced torture often face specific barriers when seeking international protection. The physical and psychological consequences of torture can shape how someone is able to recount and substantiate what happened to them. Medical evidence and a better understanding of the visible and less visible consequences of torture can be decisive in these cases, but only if it is properly gathered and given appropriate weight.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
328 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 138 0 R
/T (NANSEN's work with survivors of torture centres on individual legal assistance: helping survivors document their injuries and the consequences of ill-treatment through specialised medical examinations and reports, prepared in line with the Istanbul Protocol. This work is funded by the United Nations Voluntary Fund for Victims of Torture \(UNVFVT\). We monitor whether and how the asylum authorities take medical evidence into account when assessing protection claims and track the identification of special procedural needs. As an implementing partner of UNHCR, NANSEN can intervene directly with the CGRS, in addition to working with lawyers, to help ensure that procedures are adapted to the specific needs of applicants where possible. )
/E (NANSEN's work with survivors of torture centres on individual legal assistance: helping survivors document their injuries and the consequences of ill-treatment through specialised medical examinations and reports, prepared in line with the Istanbul Protocol. This work is funded by the United Nations Voluntary Fund for Victims of Torture \(UNVFVT\). We monitor whether and how the asylum authorities take medical evidence into account when assessing protection claims and track the identification of special procedural needs. As an implementing partner of UNHCR, NANSEN can intervene directly with the CGRS, in addition to working with lawyers, to help ensure that procedures are adapted to the specific needs of applicants where possible. )
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [1]
>>
