1523 0 obj
<<
/Title (Behind every procedure is a person with their own needs and vulnerabilities, navigating an increasingly complex system focused on efficiency and speed.)
/Dest [13 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1521 0 R
/Next 1527 0 R
>>
endobj
1524 0 obj
<<
/Title (As a centre of legal expertise on international protection and statelessness, NANSEN works to ensure that quality legal assistance reaches the people who need it most. We look closely at each person's situation, recognising that everyone may experience visible or less visible vulnerabilities, and draw on an interdisciplinary approach to understand and dismantle the barriers they face.)
/Dest [13 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1521 0 R
/Prev 1528 0 R
>>
endobj
1525 0 obj
<<
/Title <>
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 1521 0 R
/Next 1529 0 R
>>
endobj
1526 0 obj
<<
/Title (Survivors of torture)
/Dest [21 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 1530 0 R
/First 1531 0 R
/Last 1532 0 R
/Count -2
/Next 1522 0 R
>>
endobj
1527 0 obj
<<
/Title (Founded in 2017 by a group of lawyers and academics with expertise in refugee, human rights and migration law, NANSEN aims to develop and make available high-quality legal assistance for people in need of international protection and people affected by statelessness, contributing to the effective protection of their fundamental rights.)
/Dest [13 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1521 0 R
/Prev 1523 0 R
/Next 1528 0 R
>>
endobj
1528 0 obj
<<
/Title (The need for this work remains clear. In 2025, 34,439 people applied for international protection in Belgium.1 Behind each application is a person navigating a legal and administrative system that can be difficult to understand, increasingly complex and constantly evolving. At the same time, the political and public space for a welcoming and human-centred approach to international protection is shrinking. These procedures come on top of the everyday challenges faced by persons forced to flee, including the many forms of trauma that displacement may entail.  People affected by statelessness face distinct, but equally significant, challenges in establishing their status, accessing protection and a durable residency status, and enjoying their rights.)
/Dest [13 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1521 0 R
/Prev 1527 0 R
/Next 1524 0 R
>>
endobj
1529 0 obj
<<
/Title (Detention)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 1525 0 R
/Next 1533 0 R
>>
endobj
1530 0 obj
<<
/Title <>
/Dest [20 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 1534 0 R
/Next 1526 0 R
>>
endobj
1531 0 obj
<<
/Title (People who have experienced torture often face specific barriers when seeking international protection. The physical and psychological consequences of torture can shape how someone is able to recount and substantiate what happened to them. Medical evidence and a better understanding of the visible and less visible consequences of torture can be decisive in these cases, but only if it is properly gathered and given appropriate weight.)
/Dest [21 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1526 0 R
/Next 1532 0 R
>>
endobj
1532 0 obj
<<
/Title (NANSEN's work with survivors of torture centres on individual legal assistance: helping survivors document their injuries and the consequences of ill-treatment through specialised medical examinations and reports, prepared in line with the Istanbul Protocol. This work is funded by the United Nations Voluntary Fund for Victims of Torture \(UNVFVT\). We monitor whether and how the asylum authorities take medical evidence into account when assessing protection claims and track the identification of special procedural needs. As an implementing partner of UNHCR, NANSEN can intervene directly with the CGRS, in addition to working with lawyers, to help ensure that procedures are adapted to the specific needs of applicants where possible.)
/Dest [21 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1526 0 R
/Prev 1531 0 R
>>
endobj
1533 0 obj
<<
/Title <>
/Dest [16 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 1529 0 R
/Next 1535 0 R
>>
endobj
1534 0 obj
<<
/Title (Beyond Belgium, NANSEN brought its expertise to the European Migration Network's multistakeholder meeting on statelessness in October 2025 and continued contributing to the European Network on Statelessness and the Statelessness Index - tracking how Belgian law and practice measure up against international standards.)
/Dest [20 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 1536 0 R
/Next 1530 0 R
>>
endobj
1535 0 obj
<<
/Title <>
/Dest [16 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 1533 0 R
/Next 1537 0 R
>>
endobj
1536 0 obj
<<
/Title (The EU Pact on Migration and Asylum also raises important questions for stateless people. NANSEN analysed its implications for Belgium and put forward concrete recommendations to ensure that statelessness is properly identified and registered. As the Pact is implemented, NANSEN will closely monitor how these safeguards are applied in practice and whether they contribute to stronger protection of stateless people.)
/Dest [20 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 1538 0 R
/Next 1534 0 R
>>
endobj
1537 0 obj
<<
/Title (Sexual orientation and/or gender identity)
